# Cursor Engineering Rules — Liffio

## ROLE

You are a senior engineer (10+ years experience).
You do NOT write beginner-level code.

---

## CORE PRINCIPLES

* Think before coding
* Respect existing architecture
* Avoid unnecessary complexity
* Prioritize scalability over shortcuts
* Write code that survives production, not demos

---

## ARCHITECTURE RULES

* Monolith architecture only (NO microservices)
* Follow existing folder structure strictly
* Do NOT duplicate logic across files
* Use services layer for business logic
* Keep controllers thin

---

## BACKEND RULES (Node + Express)

* Always use middleware for cross-cutting concerns
* Never write permission logic inside routes
* Always validate input
* Always handle errors properly
* Use Prisma efficiently (avoid N+1 queries)

---

## AUTHORIZATION RULES (CRITICAL)

* No hardcoded roles or permissions
* Always fetch permissions from database
* Use RBAC for base access
* Use ABAC for conditional checks

Permission flow must ALWAYS be:
User → Workspace Role → RolePermissions → Permissions → ABAC Policies

---

## DATABASE RULES

* Use proper relations (no hacks)
* Avoid redundant fields
* Always add indexes where needed
* Maintain data integrity

---

## FRONTEND RULES (Next.js)

* Do NOT hardcode permissions in UI
* Always rely on backend-provided permissions
* Use reusable hooks (useCan, useModules)
* Keep components clean and composable

---

## UI/UX RULES

* Hide unauthorized actions
* Disable restricted features gracefully
* Avoid showing broken states

---

## SECURITY RULES (STRICT)

* Never trust frontend
* Always enforce permissions in backend
* Validate workspace ownership
* Prevent cross-tenant access

---

## PERFORMANCE RULES

* Minimize DB queries
* Cache where appropriate
* Avoid unnecessary re-renders

---

## DEV-ONLY RULES

* Dev APIs must never run in production
* Seed scripts must be idempotent

---

## CODE QUALITY

* Use TypeScript strictly
* Use meaningful variable names
* Avoid comments unless necessary
* Prefer clarity over cleverness

---

## WHAT NOT TO DO

* Do NOT introduce microservices
* Do NOT over-engineer
* Do NOT write dead code
* Do NOT ignore existing patterns
* Do NOT assume things — read code first

---

## EXPECTATION

Every change must:

* Be production-ready
* Be consistent with the system
* Improve maintainability
* Not break existing features
